            <ArticleTitle>[Pain in patients with opiates dependence].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1221-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.lpm.2012.07.030</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0755-4982(12)00511-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We have to trust the patient. Pain is undertreated. Inadequate treatment has adverse consequences. Over and under treatment are both liabilities. Opioid-maintained patients do experience pain. These patients report higher pain scores. They experience both opioid tolerance and hyperalgesia. They are responsive to opioid analgesia. They need baseline opioids (&quot;opioid debt&quot;), mostly a well-stabilized OST. They require higher doses, more frequent dosing and longer opioid administration.</AbstractText>
